.TH UCC 1 "2026-11-12" "ucc 0.9.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ucc \- emit Unicode characters by name, codepoint, or alias
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ucc
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " NAME"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ucc
looks up a Unicode character and writes it to standard output as UTF-8,
without a trailing newline.
Quote names containing spaces so that the shell passes them as one argument.
.PP
Lookup is case-insensitive, in this order: exact user aliases, direct
codepoints, exact character-map names and aliases, then fuzzy matches.
Codepoints accept hexadecimal (for example, 2014), U+2014, or 0x2014.
Invalid Unicode scalar values, including surrogates, are rejected.
Direct codepoints may emit characters outside the printable character map.
.PP
Fuzzy lookup accepts substrings or word prefixes in order, with intervening
words allowed. For example, SUBSCR ONE matches SUBSCRIPT ONE, and LAT CAP A
matches LATIN CAPITAL LETTER A.
Word order matters; lookup does not correct typos.
Names and aliases referring to the same character count as one result.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-pager ,
an ambiguous lookup reports up to ten matches on standard error and fails.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-charmap " FILE"
Use an alternative character map CSV.
The header must contain na, cp, na1, and aliases columns.
The cp column contains hexadecimal codepoints; aliases contains JSON arrays
of names. Fuzzy lookup requires a seekable file.
.TP
.BI \-\-aliases " FILE"
Use an alternative alias CSV instead of the default user alias file.
An explicitly specified file must exist.
.TP
.BI \-\-combine " NAME"
Append another character using the same lookup rules as the main argument.
Repeat this option to append more characters in order.
Characters may be ordinary letters or combining marks; no Unicode
normalization or rearrangement is performed.
Rendering depends on the terminal and font.
.TP
.B \-\-pager
List all matching characters on standard output, one per line, in the form
<char> (<name>). Uses the same lookup precedence as ordinary lookup.
Does not start a pager; pipe the output to
.BR less (1)
or another pager.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-combine .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option processing. The remaining argument is treated as NAME even if it
begins with a hyphen.
.SH ALIASES
Alias files are UTF-8 CSV files without a header, with two fields per row:
name and value. A value may be a literal Unicode character or a hexadecimal
codepoint. A single Unicode scalar value is always interpreted as a literal;
use U+0041 to specify the codepoint rather than the literal A.
Names are case-insensitive; later duplicate aliases replace earlier ones.
Quote CSV fields containing commas or quotes.
.PP
Example alias file:
.PP
.nf
dash,2014
letter,A
accent,U+0301
.fi
.PP
A missing default alias file is ignored. Invalid alias files are errors.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ucc/aliases.csv
Default user aliases when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is nonempty.
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/ucc/aliases.csv
Default user aliases when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset or empty.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/ucc/printable.csv
Default character map for a source installation.
Release packages use
.IR /usr/share/ucc/printable.csv .
The default directory is compiled into the binary and may differ for custom
installations. Use
.B \-\-charmap
to override it at runtime.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " HOME
Determine the default alias path as described under FILES.
.PP
UCC_DATADIR selects the default character-map directory at build time;
setting it at runtime has no effect.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful character output, match listing, help, or version request.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments, a missing or ambiguous character, invalid input data,
or an input/output error. Diagnostics are written to standard error.
A failed character lookup emits no partial character sequence.
With
.BR \-\-pager ,
multiple matches are successful, but no matches is an error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Emit an em dash:
.PP
.nf
ucc 'EM DASH'
.fi
.PP
Use a codepoint in shell output, adding a newline with printf:
.PP
.nf
printf '%s\en' "$(ucc U+2192)"
.fi
.PP
Append a combining accent:
.PP
.nf
ucc 'LATIN SMALL LETTER E' \e
  \-\-combine 'COMBINING ACUTE ACCENT'
.fi
.PP
Browse matching arrows:
.PP
.nf
ucc ARROW \-\-pager | less
.fi
.PP
Use the example alias file:
.PP
.nf
ucc \-\-aliases ./aliases.csv letter \-\-combine accent
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR less (1),
.BR printf (1)
